# =============================================================================
# PETRICHOR — .gitignore
# =============================================================================
# A Unity repository ignores generated state, not source. The rule of thumb:
# if Unity can regenerate it from the assets and the manifest, it does not
# belong in git. Everything under Assets/ and Packages/ does.
#
# See CONTRIBUTING.md section 6 for the LFS policy that pairs with this file.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Unity generated state
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Library/ is the imported asset cache. It is large, machine-specific, and
# fully regenerable. Committing it is the single most common Unity mistake.
/Library/
/Temp/
/Obj/
/Logs/
/UserSettings/
/MemoryCaptures/
/Recordings/

# Build output. Never committed — CI produces it, and it is large.
/[Bb]uild/
/[Bb]uilds/
/[Ll]ogs/
/[Uu]ser[Ss]ettings/

# Unity's crash and diagnostic dumps.
sysinfo.txt
*.stackdump
crashlytics-build.properties

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Addressables generated data
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ServerData is the built bundle output that gets uploaded to a CDN. The group
# assets that DESCRIBE it are committed; the built output is not.
/[Aa]ssets/AddressableAssetsData/ServerData/
/[Aa]ssets/AddressableAssetsData/*/*.bin
/[Aa]ssets/AddressableAssetsData/AssetGroups/*.asset.meta.tmp

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Benchmark output
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The verdict file IS committed (it is a project decision record). The raw
# per-run samples are not: they are large, machine-specific, and regenerated
# by every run.
/BenchmarkResults/*
!/BenchmarkResults/verdict_*.txt
!/BenchmarkResults/.gitkeep

# Python bytecode. Tools/check_thresholds.py is run from the shell rather than
# imported, so a __pycache__ here only ever appears from a syntax check
# (python -m py_compile) - it is not a build product, and committing it would
# add a file that changes on every interpreter patch release.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. IDE and editor state
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.csproj
*.unityproj
*.sln
*.suo
*.tmp
*.user
*.userprefs
*.pidb
*.booproj
*.svd
*.pdb
*.mdb
*.opendb
*.VC.db

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. OS noise
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Project-specific
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local tier overrides. The committed tier data lives in Assets/Data/Tiers/;
# this is for a developer's machine-specific override.
/TierOverride.local.json

# Local Unity licence and machine config.
#
# *.ulf  the manual-activation licence. This is the format the CI gate
#        needs, and it is a credential: it must never be committed.
# *.ulm  the legacy machine licence.
# *.alf  the activation REQUEST file. It is not a credential by itself, but
#        it is machine-bound and useless in the repository.
# *.txml the extension Unity Hub uses for a licence it exports. It is a
#        licence file whatever its extension, so it is ignored for the same
#        reason as *.ulf.
#
# The entitlement licence is named UnityEntitlementLicense.xml and lives in
# the Licensing Client's own directory. It is matched by name rather than by
# a blanket *.xml rule: a Unity project legitimately contains .xml files
# (package documentation, Android manifests, Addressables config), and
# ignoring all of them would silently drop real source.
*.ulf
*.ulm
*.alf
*.txml
UnityEntitlementLicense.xml

# Purchased art staging. Art is purchased rather than authored, and the raw
# vendor downloads are large and re-downloadable. The imported assets under
# Assets/Art/ ARE committed (via LFS).
/_ArtStaging/

# Local scratch for the water solver's tuning runs.
/_SolverScratch/
